{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "\"the social network\" isn't just a movie; it's a meticulously crafted, sharply observed character study wrapped in a compelling narrative of ambition, betrayal, and the dawn of the social media age. directed by david fincher, the film masterfully blends dramatic tension with a detached, almost clinical aesthetic, creating a sense of unease that permeates the entire viewing experience.\n\nthe story centers on mark zuckerberg ( 제시카 차스테인), a socially awkward and brilliant harvard student who, along with his roommate eduardo salas (아드리아노 노브라), creates facebook. the film meticulously charts the rapid rise of facebook from a simple online platform to a global phenomenon, exploring the complex relationships and power struggles that fueled its growth. it’s a fascinating look at the entrepreneurial spirit, but also a cautionary tale about the cost of success.\n\nthe performances are uniformly excellent. 제시카 차스테인 delivers a nuanced portrayal of zuckerberg, capturing his intelligence, arrogance, and underlying vulnerability. her portrayal is subtle yet powerful, allowing the audience to understand the man behind the algorithm. 아드리아노 노브라 provides a contrasting foil to zuckerberg, embodying the idealistic and somewhat naive vision of facebook's early days. the supporting cast, including 조던 피텔 ( 레이치엘 매든) and 벤 아플렉 ( 에릭 슈메이거), are equally strong, each contributing to the film’s rich tapestry of characters.\n\nfincher's direction is exceptional. he utilizes a distinctive visual style – employing close-ups, fragmented camera angles, and a predominantly cool color palette – to reflect the characters' emotional states and the rapidly evolving nature of the technology. the soundtrack, composed by ben bishop, is equally effective, enhancing the film’s mood and creating a sense of both exhilaration and impending doom.\n\nthe film's script, written by adam mckay, is sharp and 